    {
      "id": 19,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "title": "READ ME FIRST",
      "pos": [
        1700,
        0
      ],
      "size": [
        620,
        980
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 18,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Read me first\n\n**SageAttention comes from the launch flag, not from a node.**\n\n    python main.py --use-sage-attention\n\n`wrap_attn` then passes SageAttention to the override as `func`\n(`comfy/ldm/modules/attention.py:193`), so it becomes what `SolAttnH3` falls\nback to and what recomputes the sink's query rows. Without the flag you get\nPyTorch SDPA, roughly 2.5x slower at these shapes.\n\n**Do not add `Patch Sage Attention KJ` to this graph.** It assigns\n`model_options[\"transformer_options\"][\"optimized_attention_override\"]` — the\nsame key `SolAttnH3` uses. They overwrite each other, the last node in the\nchain wins, and the loser does nothing at all: no warning, no log line, just\nthe speedup quietly gone.\n\n**Confirm it is running.** Once per run the console prints:\n\n    [sol-attn-h3] SM120, CuTe DSL available, backend=cute_sm120, DiT blocks: 50\n    [sol-attn-h3] correctness gate PASS ...\n    [sol-attn-h3] routing density {... 'effective_density': 0.25 ...}\n\n`backend=triton` on an SM89+ card means the CuTe runtime is missing.\n`sparse_calls: 0` means every call declined — the reason is in `declined`.\n\n**Frame length** snaps up to the model's 17k+5 grid: 124 (~5 s), 141, 158, …,\n362 (~15 s). Off-grid values are rounded up silently, so 125 gives you 141.\n\n### Reference images\n\nSockets grow one at a time: connected ones plus a single free slot per group.\n`ref_audio_1` is not missing — it appears once `ref_audio_0` is filled. The node\ntakes 9 images, 3 reference videos, 3 soundtracks for those videos and 3\nstandalone audios.\n\n**Connect them in the order your `<Picture N>` tags use** — `<Picture 1>` is\n`ref_image_0`. The tokenizer presents references in connection order, so a\nmismatch points your prompt at the wrong image without complaining. Three or\nfour varied shots hold identity far better than one.\n\n`ref_image_size='match'` scales each reference to the generation's pixel area.\n`'max'` uses the reference pipeline's 2048 px short edge for the best identity\nfidelity and is several times slower — reference tokens ride through **every**\nsampling step, not just the first.\n\n### Length and whether the node pays\n\nThis graph ships at 124, which on the i2v sibling is the length where the node\ndid *not* pay (0.90x there, against 1.10x at 362). Reference images make the\nsink larger than a single first frame, so ref2va should want the longer length\neven more — **but that is reasoning from the i2v measurement, not a measurement\nof this graph.** 124 is the default here only because it is the safe one for\nVRAM; 362 plus reference tokens on a 16 GB card is untested.\n"
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "text": "## Read me first\n\n**SageAttention comes from the launch flag, not from a node.**\n\n    python main.py --use-sage-attention\n\n`wrap_attn` then passes SageAttention to the override as `func`\n(`comfy/ldm/modules/attention.py:193`), so it becomes what `SolAttnH3` falls\nback to and what recomputes the sink's query rows. Without the flag you get\nPyTorch SDPA, roughly 2.5x slower at these shapes.\n\n**Do not add `Patch Sage Attention KJ` to this graph.** It assigns\n`model_options[\"transformer_options\"][\"optimized_attention_override\"]` — the\nsame key `SolAttnH3` uses. They overwrite each other, the last node in the\nchain wins, and the loser does nothing at all: no warning, no log line, just\nthe speedup quietly gone.\n\n**Confirm it is running.** Once per run the console prints:\n\n    [sol-attn-h3] SM120, CuTe DSL available, backend=cute_sm120, DiT blocks: 50\n    [sol-attn-h3] correctness gate PASS ...\n    [sol-attn-h3] routing density {... 'effective_density': 0.25 ...}\n\n`backend=triton` on an SM89+ card means the CuTe runtime is missing.\n`sparse_calls: 0` means every call declined — the reason is in `declined`.\n\n**Frame length** snaps up to the model's 17k+5 grid: 124 (~5 s), 141, 158, …,\n362 (~15 s). Off-grid values are rounded up silently, so 125 gives you 141.\n\n### Reference images\n\nSockets grow one at a time: connected ones plus a single free slot per group.\n`ref_audio_1` is not missing — it appears once `ref_audio_0` is filled. The node\ntakes 9 images, 3 reference videos, 3 soundtracks for those videos and 3\nstandalone audios.\n\n**Connect them in the order your `<Picture N>` tags use** — `<Picture 1>` is\n`ref_image_0`. The tokenizer presents references in connection order, so a\nmismatch points your prompt at the wrong image without complaining. Three or\nfour varied shots hold identity far better than one.\n\n`ref_image_size='match'` scales each reference to the generation's pixel area.\n`'max'` uses the reference pipeline's 2048 px short edge for the best identity\nfidelity and is several times slower — reference tokens ride through **every**\nsampling step, not just the first.\n\n### Length and whether the node pays\n\nThis graph ships at 124, which on the i2v sibling is the length where the node\ndid *not* pay (0.90x there, against 1.10x at 362). Reference images make the\nsink larger than a single first frame, so ref2va should want the longer length\neven more — **but that is reasoning from the i2v measurement, not a measurement\nof this graph.** 124 is the default here only because it is the safe one for\nVRAM; 362 plus reference tokens on a 16 GB card is untested.\n"
      },
